  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - Central Idea: Implement a novel combination of multi-resolution analysis with Bayesian optimization and response surface methodology to systematically search for the globally optimal solution, leveraging hierarchical structure discovery and probabilistic optimization to escape local minima.\n   - Key Rationale: This differs from previous attempts by:\n     1. Integrating multiple optimization techniques (Bayesian, gradient-based, local search) into a cohesive framework with dynamic resolution adaptation\n     2. Using adaptive smoothing of the non-convex objective to enable gradient-based methods\n     3. Incorporating a learned surrogate model to efficiently explore the high-dimensional space\n   - Bottlenecks Addressed: Local optima avoidance, black-box optimization, expensive function evaluations, and inefficient use of computational budget through:\n     - Multi-resolution progression from coarse to fine grids\n     - Strategic use of Bayesian optimization for global search phases\n     - Nimble switching between gradient-based and direct search methods based on progress\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: n-point samples (even n) over [0,2] with \u0394x=2/n\n   - Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n with exactly sum(h)\u00b7\u0394x = 1\n   - Objective: C\u2085 = (1/\u0394x) \u00b7 max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Smoothed Objective for Optimization:\n     f_\u03b1(h) = (1/\u03b1)log(\u2211_{k\u2208K} exp(\u03b1(c_k - m))) + \u03bc(x\u00b7\u0394x - 1)\u00b2\n     where:\n       - m = max_{k\u2208K} c_k\n       - c_k = \u0394x \u00b7 \u2211h_i\u00b7(1-h_{i+(k)_p}) \n       - K is a neighborhood of top-correlation lags \n       - \u03b1 is temperature (100-500000)\n       - \u03bc is regularization (0-0.1)\n   - Projection: Dynamically clip values to [0,1] then force integral to exactly 1\n   - Dimensionality: Valid snippet handling from 32-1024 points supported\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Global Initialization:\n     def run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, n_final=None):\n       t0 = time.time()\n       deadline = t0 + budget_s - 300\n       best_solution = None\n       best_c5 = float('inf')\n       n_grid = n_final if n_final is not None else None\n\n       # Multi-resolution phases:\n       s = time.time()\n       # Phase1: Coarse resolution search (75-100ms)\n       if n_grid < 32 or n_grid is None: \n           n_grids = [32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024]\n       else:\n           n_grids = [n_grid]\n\n       # Phase2: Bayesian optimization setup (250ms)\n       for fit in n_grids:\n           if time.time() > deadline: break\n           optimize_with_target_resolution(fit)\n           best_solution, best_c5 = update_best_candidate()\n\n       # Phase3: Progressive fine-tuning (most time)\n       if time.time() < deadline-300:\n           adaptive_budget_optimization(best_solution, best_c5)\n\n       # Phase4: Validation\n       validate_candidate(best_solution)\n\n       # Return required format\n       return best_solution, best_c5, n_grid\n\n   - Core Optimizer:\n      def bayesian_lbfgs_search():\n        initialize_gp_model(initial_sample_size=5)\n        while time_left():\n            acquisition_max = bayesian_optimization_acquisition()\n            perturb_point = select_point_to_perturb(acquisition_max)\n            new_x = lbfgs_step(perturb_point,)\n            score = evaluate_fitness(new_x)\n            update_gp_model(new_x, score)\n            if improvement_significant: best_c5 = score\n\n   - Full Procedure:\n     Define grid, force finite, if no parent then evaluate all lags, else use parent as seed\n     Start with Bayesian optimization at coarse grid\n     Implement multi-level warmstart with projections\n     iGP_model for high-dimensional space reduction\n     Local gradient explosions handled via trust-region clipping\n     Automatic dimensionality reduction as \u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i becomes positive/negative\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Dimensions:\n     Base n from 32 to 1024 (doubling strategy)\n     Estimate computation cost: O(n log\u00b2n) per forward pass\n   - Bayesian Optimization:\n     Use Expected Improvement (EI) acquisition function\n     Initialize with 5-10 random points per level\n     Tweak temperature \u03b1 dynamically: \u03b1 = \u03bb * (1 - exp(-t/T))\n   - Optimization Methods:\n     Default: L-BFGS-B for smooth regions, with:\n        tol = 1e-8\n        max_iter = min(1000, 100*n)\n        ftol = sqrt(\u03b5)\n     Fallback: Stochastic gradient descent with adaptive learning\n   - Early Termination:\n     - If score stabilizes (no change for 30 iterations)\n     - If wall time approaching remaining budget\n     - If variance in subspace decays below (budget\u00b7\u03b5)\n   - Dimensionality Handling:\n     n_eff = n/np.log(np.sqrt(np.exp(\u03b5)) + 2\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Estimations (budget/time scaling tests):\n     Coarse grid (32 pts): 42ms\n     Medium grid (256 pts): 480ms\n     Fine grid (1k+pts): 1.4s\n   - Dominant costs: Lag evaluation (O(n), correlation (O(n\u00b2)), projection (O(n))\n   - Scheduling (examples):\n     25% budget: Grid selection phase\n     50% budget: Coarse Bayesian phase\n     25% budget: Fine-tuning phase\n   - Fallback strategy:\n     50 ms: Cohesive descent (gradient averaging)\n     75 ms: Dimensionality recovery\n     90 ms: Stochastic annealing recovery\n   - Numerical Safeguards:\n     - Handle sum constraints via projection matrix\n     - Clip inv\u00e1lido scores using robust statistics\n     - Fallback function to nearest parent solution\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Feasibility Checks:\n     0 <= h[i] <= 1 (by clipping)\n     abs(sum(h)\u00b7\u0394x - 1) < 1e-6\u00b7sum(h)\n   - Score Calculation:\n     c5 = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (\u0394x)\n   - Score Verification:\n     Primary: Fast smooth approximation\n     Secondary: Exact numerical integration (fallback)\n   - Performance Boundaries:\n     Validation interval: 1e-12 <= c5 <= 0.38\n     If stuck, epsilon-greedy restart (1 % probability)\n   - Data Handling:\n     Expand grid as needed, preserve sum, rotate gradients\n     Handle arbitrary grid sizes through interpolation\n   - Final Output Format:\n     (h_array, c5_value, n_points)\n   - Special Checks:\n     - Saturation threshold: if all lags very close, use stochastic gatekeeping\n     - Handle invalid grid values via repair matrices\n     - Latitude restrictions for shifts requiring extrapolation",
